r okay very good  how did you come to write the book what  was your inspiration um started in  graduate school so I did my PhD at the  University of Chicago and I was working  on international security topics  looking for answer tation topic and I  was struck by two things one well first  of all it's for me it was the most  important topic I could think of in the  sense that you know polarity is the way  the world works today so it's the way  the international system works today  it's if you want the background  condition on which states make their  decisions and looking at what had been  written on the topic a great deal has  been written on the topic I'm by no  means the first one to touch it I was  dissatisfied with how the literature  just focused on great power interactions  and so basically the summary of the  literature I could find was well we had  two great powers during the Cold War one  is gone the Soviet Union therefore  there's only one therefore it's peaceful  there can't be conflict because there's  only one and of course this is right  it's also I believe quite trivial and so  I started to look into interactions  between the one great power we have the  United States and other states and the  second point that that got me very  interested in this was that since the  typical view that you get published on  the topic is that you know polarity is  indeed peaceful I was struck by the fact  that the US has been involved in a great  deal of war since the end of the Cold  War so the u.s. in the first 200 years  of existence in which it was one among  several great powers was it worked for  35 years in the last 20 years since in  the Cold War it has been at war for 12  so more than half and it struck me that  there was a lot of room for improving  our understanding of the dynamics for  that generate conflict in worlds in  which there's no other great power how  did you do the research for your book  I'm still doing it it's it's mostly  research on the theoretical aspects of  it so first of all because I don't  believe we have empirical evidence to  prove a theory right or wrong we only  have 20 years of you know polarity we  haven't had any system like this before  we had great hegemonic powers in the  past like Rome Imperial Rome but they  worked in ways that were very different  we didn't have a state system so they  had an imperial dimension that we don't  have today and so it's it's a rather  unique situation and the the empirical  evidence we  is scant we have 20 years of evidence I  don't think with that you can prove any  theory right or wrong and so I thought  the burden was on the theoretical  development and sometimes people tell me  well if you can't prove the theory right  or wrong so why is it that you're  devoting yourself to the topic and I'd  go back and say that is the most  important topic I can see today in in  international affairs and so I started  by looking at the theories that exist  out there that could help us deal with  with this system that we have today with  figuring out how the world works with  only one great power and I didn't find  much about interactions between that  great power and other states so I  started to explore that and also with a  great deal of attention to the question  of nuclear proliferation because I think  that nuclear weapons make a great deal  of difference in terms of states being  able to defend themselves without  necessarily acquiring the same power say  the US has so states today can sort of  defend themselves in a relatively  cheaper way by acquiring the nuclear  arsenal without developing all the  conventional power that the US has so I  started to look at in terms of the  question of peace which was the first  one I tried to address how different the  world is today in terms of smaller  powers not having a potential security  sponsor okay so it's a very different  world from say the Cold War in which if  a state felt threatened by the US  it could very likely find a sponsor in  the Soviet Union patron and Alliance  supporter these days that option is not  there and so by definition they can't  seize there's only one great power they  can't find another great power to sort  of prevent an attack or a threat from  this great power and therefore I think  that there's a great incentive for them  to acquire the capabilities the military  capabilities namely nuclear weapons that  would guarantee their security and  survival ok and why is it important to  look at you know polarity especially in  terms of the three questions you put  forth  so the topic is important I think  because not only it's the way the world  works today it will be the way the world  will work as far as the eye can see so  we will live in a unipolar world  probably for my lifetime at least four  three four decades hopefully my lifetime  is longer but at least four three four  that gets more and so it's imperative  that those of us who study international  politics actually devote themselves to  studying the dynamics that will take  place in these next few decades many  people today think that the u.s. is in  decline  you have successive waves of what I call  decline is literature in the eighties  there was the fury of Japan rising today  there's the the idea that China will  rise and take over and so I think that  it's recalled of Niels Bohr the  prominent physicist saying that  prediction is a very difficult business  particularly about the future I can't  really predict whether China is going to  rise or not but I think whether China  rises economically is not necessary it's  not there's will not necessarily lead to  China rising militarily it depends on  how we deal with them so I think it's  important because we may despite the  rise of states that are growing faster  because they are at a sort of earlier  stage in their development like China  India Brazil  perhaps Russia will recover who knows  despite that we will still have a long  period in which we will live now in  terms of the three questions I asked  them do you just ask me back I think  those are peace and durability are the  most important questions you can ask so  if you believe that the system is  peaceful you want the system to be  durable if you believe the system is not  peaceful you don't want the system to be  durable so they're interrelated and  those the answers of the answer to those  two questions depends the amount of  conflict and the costs and benefits that  actually being a unipolar power being  the number one power in the world by a  very great different difference like the  u.s. is today the benefits and the costs  of that so whether in fact it's a good  thing that the u.s. is number one  and how much conflict can we expect as a  result of that situation and then I look  at the smaller question of deterrence  the narrower question of deterrence  because deterrence is is a literature  that emerged in the Cold War and it's a  technical term but basically mean means  how do States get to go to war so if  there is a crisis between two states  what has to happen for that crisis to  actually result in military conflict and  so it's sort of the as we call it the  micro foundations for answering the  other two questions so the micro answer  for the other two questions that's why  it okay and what are some of the  conclusions you reach in your book so  first conclusion which is about the  first question is that any polarity is  not peaceful so I don't necessarily  disagree with the conventional wisdom  that it is peaceful in what concerns  conflict about great powers because of  course there's only one so by definition  it's going to be peaceful I do disagree  however in that it is peaceful overall  because I think the effect of a unipolar  structure of the international system on  the interactions between the great power  of the US and minor powers may result in  a level of conflict that will compensate  in a negative way that absence of great  power war now it's very difficult to  give more precise answers in terms of  how many casualties can you expect how  big will those conflicts go the war is  very unpredictable so once you get into  conflict I I bracket that question but  it's likely that we will have  significant confident levels of conflict  the other aspect the other question is  the question of durability which is a  question on which there is great debate  will it be durable will the u.s. be  forever the only great power will it be  the only great power only for a while is  it an age and then China or India or  whoever it is takes over or is it  actually a moment that's already  finishing because of these days the  financial crisis you know there's always  a temptation of sort of finding the news  of the day and turning them into an  argument for us decline I think that  question has two answers and the witcha  to focus is on the first the people tend  to look at  economic growth and of course since the  u.s. is not the most populous country in  the world as other countries catch up in  terms of economic development their  economy will be larger and it's very  likely that the US will not be the  largest economy in the world three four  five several decades ahead but I think  the rich term which is the second step  which is well will that economic power  rising economic power that other country  necessarily convert that economic power  into military power and compete with the  US and try to for instance deny the u.s.  the current command it has over globally  air sea space the u.s. currently has  control of what we call the comments  everything that's not another state is  controlled by the US and it has around a  thousand military installations abroad  would a rising China try to revert that  situation and I think that the answer to  that is it depends is the answer to many  questions is depends on the actions of  the u.s. it depends it's very much in  the u.s. is own hands to have a policy  of accommodation and reconciliation that  enables the rising power to actually  feel satisfied with the system to  extract from the system the benefits it  fuels it is entitled to as a result of  its rising power or whether the US will  try to contain a rising power and  therefore increase its dissatisfaction  with the way the system works and as a  result lead it to actually convert its  latent power economic power into  military power and try to compete with  the u.s. so I think that depends very  much on how the US will behave in the  next few decades okay final question and  you've touched on that a little bit but  how does your theory of unipolarity  differ from conventional wisdom today  three points first I would say I try to  argue that as I said the new clarity  will be the way the world will work for  a long time whereas I think the  consensus today not the consensus but  the growing trend in the literature is  to find reasons for u.s. decline be it  the financial crisis  that somehow will affect the u.s. more  than it affects other countries be it's  military over extension the fact that  we're fighting a number of conflicts all  over the world through conflicts and  that we are overextended those conflicts  are not going particularly well at least  Afghanistan and our military budgets and  all of that are overstretched to a very  old older and significant argument by  Paul Kennedy my colleague here that says  you know great powers tend to overextend  and therefore the Cline  the growing consensus is on that  position I would say that that's not  necessarily the case that you could see  the same preoccupation with the rising  Soviet Union in the 50s and 60s you  could see the same preoccupation with  the rising of Japan in the 80s huge  literature saying we're doomed they're  gonna overtake us in five years allows  they didn't grow in the 90s or in the  past decade very little growth so that  question I think is I'm more inclined to  say we will have unipolarity for a  longer time and most people believe and  I would also differ on the question of  peace as I said not because people are  wrong but because they're not looking at  what I think they should look at which  is the same dynamics between the US and  countries like Iran or Korea arguably  those will be smaller conflicts than a  world war will be or a war with Russia  World War three with the Soviet Russia  but they can still be important  conflicts and those are mechanisms for  conflict that are directly derived from  the fact that the world is unipolar so  unipolarity per se doesn't cause any war  but it creates the background conditions  that facilitate that promote certain  mechanisms that can leave to war and to  those mechanisms that I've developed my  attention in terms of the durability  question the last one and perhaps the  most important I think my take differs  in that I separate the economic growth  aspect of it from the military growth  and if perhaps we can look at the  economic growth aspect of in terms of  other powers rising and eventually  overtaking the US  economic size power I think that's a  separate question from the military  power question and I try to  differentiate between both and argue  that in a nuclear age a state can  guarantee its security and survival by  acquiring nuclear weapons without  necessarily matching the u.s. power and  that in fact if once it has acquired  nuclear weapons it's priced to continue  to acquire power to the point at which  it competes with the US it's actually  taking actions that are detrimental to  its survival and security because of the  presence of nuclear weapons so I think  those are the main differences I see  thank you very much for being here with  us today sharing some of your research  for more information about professor  Montero and his work please visit our  website at yale.edu backslash macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you

